      "id": 1,
      "text": "Collyer earned its name from Rev. Robert Collyer, whose dedication inspired settlers to honor him with the town’s name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collyer%2C_Kansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early settlers moved Collyer twice, each time shifting the town closer to reliable water sources to sustain the community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/collyer-kansas-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Union Pacific Railroad’s 1867 arrival sparked Collyer’s initial settlement, establishing water stations near the future town site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/collyer-kansas-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Collyer’s original site at Coyote was abandoned in 1879 when the Union Pacific set its permanent station there.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/collyer-kansas-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Rev. Collyer organized a lending library and built Colony House, the town’s first communal gathering spot.",
